<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Battista Tiepolo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1696">1696</a>–<a href="/years/1770">1770</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Tiepolo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Battista Tiepolo was a Venetian painter, the leading European decorative painter of the 18th century. Known for luminous ceiling frescoes filled with figures in open skies, he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> from 1750 to 1753, where he painted the staircase and Imperial Hall of the Residence, and from 1762 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-battista-tiepolo.jpg" alt="Giovanni Battista Tiepolo" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Battista Tiepolo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiepolo,_Giovanni_Battista_-_Fresken_Treppenhaus_des_W%C3%BCrzburger_Residenzschlosses,_Szenen_zur_Apotheose_des_F%C3%BCrstbischofs,_Detail_Giovanni_Battista_Tiepolo_-_1750-1753.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, Giovanni Battista Tiepolo was the youngest of six children of Domenico Tiepolo, a small shipping merchant, and Orsetta Tiepolo. His family bore the patrician name without claiming noble descent. In 1710 he became a pupil of Gregorio Lazzarini, but he was at least as strongly influenced by Sebastiano Ricci, Giovanni Battista Piazzetta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tintoretto">Tintoretto</a> and Veronese. His earliest known works are apostles painted in 1715 and 1716 at Santa Maria dei Derelitti in Venice, and he painted his first fresco in 1716 on a church ceiling at Biadene, near Treviso. In 1719 he married Maria Cecilia Guardi, sister of the painters Francesco and Giovanni Antonio Guardi. They had nine children, and two sons, Giovanni Domenico and Lorenzo, worked as his assistants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In about 1719-20 he painted the Triumph of Aurora and the Myth of Phaethon for the villa of Giambattista Baglione at Massanzago, a fluid spatial illusion that became a recurring theme. Commissions from the Dolfin family followed, including cycles for the archbishop's palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udine">Udine</a>, completed in 1726 to 1728, and ten large canvases of Roman history for Ca' Dolfin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. He used a cooler palette than earlier Venetian painters to give a convincing effect of daylight. Later works include the ceiling of the Gesuati in Venice and the story of Cleopatra at the Palazzo Labia, painted from 1745 to 1750. He also produced two sets of etchings, the Capricci and the Scherzi di fantasia.</p><p>In November 1750 he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> at the invitation of Prince-Bishop Karl Philipp von Greifenclau. He frescoed the Kaisersaal with his sons, then painted the ceiling over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann">Balthasar Neumann</a>'s staircase, finished in November 1753. This Allegory of the Planets and Continents covers 677 square metres and includes portraits of Tiepolo, his son Giandomenico and Neumann.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1753 he was elected President of the Academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and painted villas such as the Villa Valmarana and the Villa Pisani at Stra. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> commissioned him to decorate the throne room of the Royal Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> with the Apotheosis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In Spain he met the opposition of Anton Raphael Mengs, the champion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoclassicism">Neoclassicism</a>, and his canvases for the church of San Pascual at Aranjuez were replaced by works by Mengs.</p><p>Tiepolo died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 27 March 1770 and is buried in Madonna dell'Orto in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoclassicism">Neoclassicism</a> slowly ended the Rococo style associated with him but did not damage his reputation. In 1878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a> wrote in his diary, Tiepolo is my artist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giovanni Battista Tiepolo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Schönborn bishops Balthasar Neumann built the Residenz, decorated with <b>Tiepolo's</b> ceiling frescoes, and vineyards along the Main supported a wealthy wine trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg">Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1744" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Giovanni Battista Tiepolo"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5734 6163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5734 6163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1744">What was here in 1744? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
